; pi4WifiJoin.pi4 - associate with a WPA2 network and prove the link.
;
; DIAGNOSTIC. Does not ship.
;
; This is pi4WifiScan's sibling and it deliberately repeats that file's
; whole bring-up rather than sharing it. The two differ ONLY from the
; event mask onwards, and a shared bring-up would mean one file's
; failure could be caused by the other's edit - which is precisely the
; property a bring-up instrument must not have. Where a passage below is
; word-for-word pi4WifiScan's, that is on purpose and it is marked.
;
; ======================================================================
; ARM THE DEADMAN. `deadman 15` BEFORE `run`. EVERY TIME.
; ======================================================================
; The arithmetic is tighter here than in the scan, so it is worth the
; paragraph:
;
;   NOTE 2026-08-28: the nine-vector crypto block that used to run
;   before all of this is GONE from the run path - it measured 15,497 ms
;   on the board against an estimate of "about a tenth of a second", and
;   the deadman fired with the radio still cold. See section 0.
;
;   bring-up      609,312 bytes uploaded and read back      ~1 to 2 s
;   the scan      #SCAN_BUDGET_MS below                     up to 5 s
;   security      nine ioctls plus a 103 ms settle          ~0.2 s
;   the join      the library's own guards, worst case      up to 12 s
;                                                           ------------
;                                                     worst case ~19 s
;
; NINETEEN AGAINST FIFTEEN. THE WORST CASE DOES NOT FIT AND THAT IS
; STATED RATHER THAN HIDDEN, because a payload whose budget only works
; when nothing goes wrong is a payload that resets the board on the one
; run you needed to watch.
;
; WHAT THE WORST CASE ACTUALLY IS: a five-second keying stall
; (#CYW43_JOINFAIL_KEYTIMEOUT) plus the association walk. On the twin's
; bench the MEDIAN join with a clean teardown was 2677 ms and the worst
; was 4571 ms; the five-second stalls all came from an access point
; still holding a stale association for a board that had vanished while
; joined. The FIRST join after a cold radio has no such stale state to
; clear, so the expected total here is around nine seconds.
;
; AND IF IT OVERRUNS, THE DEADMAN IS THE RIGHT ANSWER rather than a
; failure of planning. The board resets itself back into the firmware
; and comes back on its own; the cost is a re-run. Unarmed, the same
; overrun wedges the board and needs somebody to reach for the mains,
; because U-Boot is gone from the stick (pi4Install.pi4:1-52). The
; elapsed-ms rows this file prints at every phase exist so that the
; nineteen above stops being arithmetic and becomes a measurement.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT TO TYPE, IN THIS ORDER
; ======================================================================
;     pmf> screen off
;     pmf> load BRCMFW.BIN  1000000
;     pmf> load BRCMNV.TXT  1200000
;     pmf> load BRCMCLM.BLB 1300000
;     pmf> fill 1400000 0 800
;     pmf> load SETTINGS.TXT 1400000
;     pmf> deadman 15
;     pmf> run 400000
;
; `screen off` IS IN THE LIST FOR A MEASURED REASON. On 2026-08-28,
; after a deadman reset, Anvil came back with the HDMI framebuffer
; enabled and the very next run of this payload could not get an answer
; out of the mailbox: `MailboxClockRate(1)` returned zero and the run
; stopped at "the firmware would not report the EMMC clock" with the
; radio never touched. pi4WifiKey.pi4 retries that query and prints
; each attempt; this file still asks once, so turn the screen off.
;
; THE `fill` LINE IS NOT OPTIONAL AND IT IS NOT TIDINESS. A payload
; takes no arguments, so it cannot be told how long SETTINGS.TXT is -
; Anvil's `load` knows and prints it, and nothing carries that number
; across the jump. Zeroing 800 hex words (8 KB) first means the file's
; last byte is followed by a NUL, so the payload can find the end by
; scanning, and a scan that finds no NUL inside the window is a REFUSAL
; rather than a guess. Skip the fill and the payload will parse the file
; plus whatever DRAM junk follows it, and settings.pi4 will refuse the
; first junk line - reporting a syntax error on a line that is not in
; the file.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE PASSPHRASE IS NOT IN THIS FILE AND MUST NEVER BE PUT IN IT
; ======================================================================
; It lives in SETTINGS.TXT on the boot medium, which is not in the
; repository, and it reaches this payload only by being read off the
; stick into DRAM by Anvil. Nothing on the host ever sees it. Type it
; once at the console with `wifi password` and `settings save`, and it
; stays on the stick.
;
; THIS FILE PRINTS THE SSID IT CHOSE, THE SLOT IT CAME FROM AND THE
; PASSPHRASE'S LENGTH - and never a byte of the passphrase itself. The
; length is the useful half: "8 characters" against a passphrase you
; know is thirteen names the fault without putting a credential in a
; serial log that ends up pasted into a report.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT SUCCESS LOOKS LIKE, IN ORDER
; ======================================================================
;   1. the scan finds a stored network, and this file names it BY BSSID
;   2. the security block is accepted - and item 2 of it is the
;      experiment that settles who runs the supplicant
;   3. association completes: SET_SSID 0, AUTH 0, LINK flags bit 0,
;      PSK_SUP 6, and the accumulator reaches $0E01
;   4. the firmware reads back a BSSID that MATCHES THE ONE WE CHOSE,
;      an SSID, a channel and an RSSI
;
; Every event is printed as it arrives, with its raw type, status,
; reason and flags, because nothing on this part has ever been measured
; and the ORDER is a bench fact this run is here to establish.
;
; ======================================================================
; THIS FILE ASSOCIATES. IT DOES NOT KEY. pi4WifiKey.pi4 DOES.
; ======================================================================
; Written 2026-08-28, once the reason for finding 3 below was settled.
; The 43455 firmware image on this bench is built WITHOUT `idsup`, the
; in-dongle supplicant - its own component list says so, beside the
; Pico's which does have it - so there is no supplicant in the radio to
; hand a passphrase to, and there never was. The four refusals recorded
; in finding 3 are one fact arriving four times.
;
; The four-way handshake therefore runs in the HOST, in
; RaspberryPi4/Lib/wpa2sup.pi4, driven by
; Examples/Diagnostics/pi4WifiKey.pi4. THIS file keeps its job: the
; scan, the selection ladder, the association and the event trace, plus
; the three questions a host supplicant needs answered (section 10b).
; It is the instrument for "did we associate and why not"; the other
; one is the instrument for "is the link keyed".
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT THIS FILE MEASURED ON 2026-08-27, ON SILICON. READ IT BEFORE
; RE-DERIVING ANY OF IT.
; ======================================================================
;
; 1. THE 2.4 GHz NETWORK ASSOCIATES. Two events, seven milliseconds
;    apart, and then four independent read-backs agreed:
;
;      [t+8054 ms] event  3 AUTH status 0 reason 0 flags $0000 state $0A01
;      [t+8061 ms] event 16 LINK status 0 reason 0 flags $0001 state $0E01
;      WLC_GET_BSSID   04:42:1A:A5:BC:90   - the BSSID we CHOSE
;      WLC_GET_SSID    ajtaji              - matches
;      WLC_GET_CHANNEL 2                   - matches the scan
;      WLC_GET_RSSI    -35 dBm             - against the scan's -33
;
;    The same ioctl answered -17 BCME_NOTASSOCIATED before the join, so
;    the second reading means something.
;
;    NOTE WHAT DID **NOT** ARRIVE: no SET_SSID status 0, and no PSK_SUP.
;    The accumulator reached $0E01 on AUTH plus LINK plus a PRESET KEYED
;    bit, and the preset is the whole reason it could. See
;    #CYW43_JOINEXP_NOKEY in cyw43.pi4. THIS LINK HAS NO PAIRWISE KEY.
;
; 2. THE 5 GHz NETWORK IS REFUSED BY THE ACCESS POINT, and the code says
;    exactly why:
;
;      [t+8094 ms] event 3 AUTH  status 0 reason 0
;      [t+8101 ms] event 7 ASSOC status 1 reason 43
;
;    43 is AKMP_NOT_VALID. Not the channel, not the signal, and NOT DFS
;    - a session was nearly spent on the DFS theory before event 7 was
;    added to the mask, and event 7 answered it in one line. The 5 GHz
;    radio does not accept the one key-management suite this driver
;    offers. It wants SAE, or PSK-SHA256 with management frame
;    protection. `bsscfg:wpa_auth` is the knob and this file has exactly
;    one value to put in it.
;
; 2b. WHAT SAE WOULD COST, WRITTEN DOWN SO NOBODY HAS TO GUESS TWICE.
;    NOT STARTED, DELIBERATELY. This is a scope note, not a plan.
;
;    THE FIRST QUESTION IS FREE AND HAS NOT BEEN ASKED. brcmfmac probes
;    the firmware for SAE with a single iovar named "sae" -
;    Reference/v6.12_brcmfmac_feature.c:45, in the same table that
;    drives every other capability - and it carries an SAE password
;    ioctl whose struct is Reference/v6.12_brcmfmac_fwil_types.h:596,
;    a length plus 128 key bytes. ONE GET_VAR ANSWERS WHETHER THIS
;    FIRMWARE HAS SAE AT ALL, and it costs one ioctl on the next bench
;    run. Everything below assumes the answer is no, because this image
;    also has no WPA2 supplicant and an image with SAE but no PSK
;    supplicant would be a very strange build. IF THE ANSWER IS YES the
;    cost collapses to sending a password and the rest of this note is
;    void, which is exactly why the question is worth one ioctl.
;
;    IF THE HOST HAS TO RUN SAE, the bill is, in order of nastiness:
;
;    a) MANAGEMENT FRAMES, AND THIS IS THE ONE THAT DECIDES IT. SAE's
;       Commit and Confirm are carried in 802.11 AUTHENTICATION frames
;       with authentication algorithm 3 - not data frames. cyw43.pi4's
;       data path (section 9) carries 802.3 frames on SDPCM channel 2
;       and cannot express an authentication frame at all. Linux does
;       external SAE by having the firmware hand AUTH frames up as
;       events and taking them back down through a vendor path; that
;       needs firmware support this image has not been shown to have.
;       If the firmware will not pass AUTH frames to the host, THERE IS
;       NO AMOUNT OF ARITHMETIC THAT MAKES SAE POSSIBLE HERE, and every
;       item below is wasted. Establish (a) before writing any of it.
;
;    b) P-256. Group 19 is the default and the only one worth doing.
;       The RP2350 tree already has the arithmetic - bignum.pico2,
;       ec256.pico2, x25519.pico2 - and none of it is ported to this
;       target. That is a real port, not a copy: RaspberryPi4/Lib's
;       sha256.pi4 header records what a 64-bit .i did to a 32-bit
;       word algorithm, and a bignum library is nothing but 32-bit
;       word assumptions.
;
;    c) HASH TO ELEMENT. WPA3 now requires H2E, which is the simplified
;       SWU map of RFC 9380 plus expand_message_xmd over SHA-256. We
;       HAVE SHA-256, HMAC-SHA256 and HKDF on this target already
;       (sha256.pi4, hmac.pi4, hkdf.pi4), so this is the cheapest item
;       on the list. The older hunting-and-pecking loop is what
;       Dragonblood broke and must not be written.
;
;    d) THE FOUR-WAY HANDSHAKE STILL HAPPENS AFTERWARDS. SAE produces a
;       PMK; everything after that is the same RSN key management
;       WPA2-PSK needs, including the AES key wrap of the GTK in
;       message 3. So SAE is strictly ADDITIONAL to the WPA2 work, not
;       an alternative route to it.
;
;    e) WPA3 MANDATES PROTECTED MANAGEMENT FRAMES, which means
;       BIP-CMAC-128 and therefore AES-CMAC, and `mfp` is one of the
;       iovars this firmware already refuses (item 7 in the security
;       block below).
;
;    THE HONEST SUMMARY: (a) is a yes/no question that costs one ioctl
;    and gates the other four items completely. Ask it before costing
;    anything else.
;
; 3. THERE IS NO SUPPLICANT IN THIS FIRMWARE. `bsscfg:sup_wpa`,
;    `bsscfg:sup_wpa2_eapver` and `bsscfg:sup_wpa_tmo` all answer -23
;    BCME_UNSUPPORTED, the bare `sup_wpa` existence probe answers -23,
;    and WLC_SET_WSEC_PMK answers -2 BCME_BADARG at BOTH documented
;    struct sizes, 68 and 132. Four independent refusals.
;
; 4. THE SELECTION RULE WAS CONSISTENT AND UNHELPFUL. With all four
;    networks stored it picked ajtaji_5G, which cannot associate; with
;    the 5 GHz slot removed it picked ajtaji, which does. The rule had
;    no retry - one choice, and it lived with it.
;
;    FIXED 2026-08-28 BY A LADDER, and the fix is not a second rule.
;    probe_BuildLadder applies the SAME three-tier rule repeatedly to
;    the candidates not yet tried, so rung 0 is exactly the choice the
;    single-choice code made and rung k is exactly what it would have
;    chosen with the first k removed. The join walks the ladder and
;    falls through ONLY on a failure a different network could fix -
;    ASSOC, PRUNE and NONET, and nothing else. See probe_FallThrough
;    for why WRONGPASS, KEYTIMEOUT and TIMEOUT are excluded, and
;    #JOIN_LADDER_BUDGET_MS for the clock that stops it.
;
;    *** NOT YET RUN ON SILICON. *** Everything above in this list was
;    measured on the board; this is not. The bench run that proves it
;    is the one where rung 0 is ajtaji_5G, the log shows status 43, and
;    rung 1 is ajtaji and associates. Until that log exists this is a
;    change that compiles.

; THE SETTINGS STORE NAMES NO OTHER FILE - house rule 10, a library never
; includes a library - so ITS dependencies are listed here, in this order,
; by the main program. Anvil/Core/settings.pi4 is chip-free core and
; reaches a medium only through the HwFile* seam: Anvil/Hal/hal.pi4
; declares that seam's #HW_FILE_* codes, and RaspberryPi4/Board/hw_file.pi4
; is THIS board's backend for it, written over fat.pi4. Both come before
; the store, exactly as the store's own USE block and
; pi4SettingsSelfTest.pi4 have it. hal.pi4's RequireCap prints a refusal
; and so names Print/PrintN/UartWriteStr, which is why uart.pi4 stays
; first.
;
; This program only ever calls SettingsParse() against the window Anvil's
; `load` filled, so none of that machinery ever executes here - but a file
; is compiled whole and every name in it has to resolve.
;
; LOSING THESE TWO LINES IS WHAT STOPPED THIS PROGRAM BUILDING. The store
; moved out of RaspberryPi4/Lib and onto the seam on 2026-09-02 (commit
; 8e17a6f1); the XIncludeFile below was given its new path and nothing
; else was added, so #HW_FILE_OK resolved nowhere in this translation
; unit. The monitor (RaspberryPi4/Board/board.pi4) and the settings gate
; (pi4SettingsSelfTest.pi4) both already listed the two files, which is
; why the identical library built there and only these two diagnostics
; broke. tools/a64/a64_diag_build_check.py builds every diagnostic in
; this directory so the next such omission is caught the same day.
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/sdio.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/cyw43_rx_glom.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/cyw43.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/fat.pi4"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Board/hw_file.pi4"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/settings.pbi"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/sha1.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/hmacsha1.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/pbkdf2.pi4"

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Where Anvil's `load` put each file. The three blob addresses, lengths
;  and CRCs are pi4WifiScan's, unchanged, and they were measured on the
;  board by reading each file back off the stick.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#FW_ADDR = $01000000
#FW_LEN  = 609309
#FW_CRC  = $B8B12CED

#NV_ADDR = $01200000
#NV_LEN  = 2074
#NV_CRC  = $2BDBDDF6

#CLM_ADDR = $01300000
#CLM_LEN  = 2676
#CLM_CRC  = $346D4B26

; SETTINGS.TXT. NO CRC, and the absence is deliberate rather than an
; oversight: the other three files are fixed vendor blobs whose bytes
; are known, and this one is edited by a person at the console. A
; constant here would fail on the first legitimate edit and would train
; whoever met it to delete the check.
;
; 8 KB of window against settings.pi4's #SET_TEXT_MAX of 6144 - the
; largest file that library will ever write - with room for the banner
; and a hand-added comment or two.
#SET_ADDR = $01400000
#SET_WIN  = 8192

; Working buffers, placed after the blobs. pi4WifiScan's, unchanged.
#NV_WORK      = $01210000
#NV_WORK_MAX  = 4096
#SCRATCH      = $01220000
#SCRATCH_LEN  = 2048

; Mailbox clock id 1 = EMMC. NOT 12, which is EMMC2's - they are
; different clocks with different rates and the wrong one produces a bus
; that almost works.
#CLOCK_ID_EMMC = 1

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The scan's budget. FIVE SECONDS HERE AND NOT THE SCAN INSTRUMENT'S
;  EIGHT, and the difference is bought rather than assumed: the measured
;  scan on this board took 56 slices of 50 ms, which is 2.8 s, so five
;  is a margin of nearly two on the observed time. The three seconds
;  saved go into the join's budget, which is the part with no headroom.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#SCAN_BUDGET_MS = 5000
#SCAN_SLICE_MS  = 50

; The join's polling slice. SHORTER THAN THE SCAN'S, because a join
; produces four to six events over several seconds rather than a burst,
; and a long slice spends most of itself waiting after the event that
; ended it has already been recorded.
#JOIN_SLICE_MS = 20

; The caller's own ceiling on the join loop, in slices. THE LIBRARY OWNS
; THE REAL GUARDS - #CYW43_JOIN_TOTAL_MS and the two phase budgets - and
; this is only the belt to their braces: a Cyw43JoinPoll that somehow
; never returned a verdict would otherwise spin until the deadman fired,
; and a deadman reset tells you nothing while a printed "the poll loop
; hit its own ceiling" tells you exactly which layer failed.
#JOIN_MAX_SLICES = 900

; ======================================================================
;  THE SELECTION RULE, AND IT IS A RULE RATHER THAN A LOOP ORDER.
;
;  PREFER 5 GHz, UNLESS IT IS TOO WEAK - THEN FALL BACK TO 2.4 GHz.
;
;  THE RULE IS NOT "PICK THE STRONGER" AND THAT DISTINCTION IS THE WHOLE
;  POINT. At this bench the 2.4 GHz network reads -27 dBm and the 5 GHz
;  one -44 dBm, so a rule that compared the two numbers would choose
;  2.4 GHz on every single run and the stated preference would never
;  once be honoured while appearing to work perfectly. The two bands are
;  NEVER compared against each other below. The 5 GHz candidate is
;  compared against a FIXED FLOOR and against nothing else.
;
;  WHY PREFER 5 GHz AT ALL: it is far less congested, its channels are
;  wider, and where it reaches at all it usually carries more. Its
;  weakness is range, which is exactly what the floor is for.
;
;  THE FLOOR IS -70 dBm AND HERE IS WHERE THAT COMES FROM. The useful
;  part of the range runs from about -30 dBm (excellent) to about
;  -80 dBm (unusable), and a WPA2 link is normally healthy down to about
;  -67 dBm, below which rate adaptation starts costing real throughput.
;  -70 sits just under that knee. It accepts the entire normal operating
;  range - this bench's -44 dBm passes with twenty-six decibels to spare
;  - and rejects only a link already into the marginal band, which is
;  the one place 2.4 GHz's better propagation is likely to win. A
;  threshold that rejected -44 would reject the normal case, which is
;  the failure to avoid.
;
;  IT IS A NAMED CONSTANT AND NOT A NUMBER IN AN `If`, because it is a
;  judgement about radio and not a fact about this code, and the next
;  person to disagree with it should be able to find it in one search.
;
;  THE BAND COMES FROM THE CONTROL CHANNEL, NOT THE CHANSPEC CENTRE.
;  Cyw43ScanChannel() is the control channel - the one the AP is
;  described as being on - and Cyw43ScanChanspecChannel() is the centre
;  of a 40 or 80 MHz block, which on this bench reads 58 for a network
;  whose control channel is 52. Channel numbers above 14 are 5 GHz.
;  Using the centre would still give the right band here, and would be
;  the wrong field for the same reason it was the wrong field in the
;  scan's channel column.
;
;  ------------------------------------------------------------------
;  RSSI IS SIGNED AND THIS FILE DOES NOT ASSUME IT SURVIVED THE TABLE.
;  ------------------------------------------------------------------
;  -44 read as an unsigned byte is 212, which is comfortably above any
;  sane threshold - so an unsigned read makes EVERY network pass the
;  floor and the fallback branch never fires, silently, for ever. The
;  sign is handled at the source (cyw43_RxI16 sign-extends a 16-bit
;  field, and cyw43_bssRssi is a 64-bit signed element), and this file
;  CHECKS THAT RATHER THAN TRUSTING IT: probe_RssiSane below refuses any
;  value outside -110..0 and the selection prints its verdict. A single
;  positive RSSI in the table would fail the run with a message naming
;  the cause, instead of quietly disabling the rule.
;
;  ------------------------------------------------------------------
;  TIES ARE BROKEN BY THE STORED SLOT, WHICH IS A PERSON'S DECISION.
;  ------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Within a band, the winner is the candidate held in the LOWEST
;  NUMBERED settings slot. That order was typed by somebody on purpose
;  and it does not change with the weather; RSSI does, and two networks
;  a decibel apart would otherwise swap places between runs and make the
;  rule unrepeatable - which is the one thing it must not be. If the
;  SAME slot matches two scan rows (one SSID, two access points) the
;  stronger RSSI wins, and if those tie too the lower BSSID wins, so
;  there is no path through this that depends on scan order.
; ======================================================================
#JOIN_5G_FLOOR_DBM = -70

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE LADDER. How many candidates the run may try, and the wall clock
;  that stops it trying more.
;
;  WHY A LADDER EXISTS AT ALL. Until 2026-08-28 the selection made ONE
;  choice and lived with it, and on this bench that choice is the 5 GHz
;  network - which refuses us with 802.11 status 43 AKMP_NOT_VALID
;  because it wants WPA3/SAE and this driver offers WPA2-PSK. The rule
;  was consistent and it picked the one network that cannot work. That
;  was recorded as finding 4 at the head of this file and deliberately
;  left standing at the time, with the note that a retry loop invented
;  at the end of a session is how a rule stops being repeatable. This
;  one is not invented at the end of a session: it reuses the SAME
;  three-tier rule, applied repeatedly to what is left, so rung 0 is
;  bit-for-bit the choice the old code made and every rung after it is
;  the choice the old code WOULD have made with the failures removed.
;
;  FOUR RUNGS, NOT "ALL OF THEM". Each rung costs an association
;  attempt, and the deadman arithmetic at the head of this file is
;  already over budget with one. Four is the number of credentials this
;  bench stores, so it is the point past which more rungs cannot help.
;
;  #JOIN_LADDER_BUDGET_MS IS THE REAL GUARD, and it is checked against
;  the run's own clock rather than against a count. A ladder bounded
;  only by a rung count can still overrun, because one slow rung costs
;  as much as three fast ones; a refusal arrives in milliseconds and a
;  timeout takes seconds, and only a clock can tell the difference
;  after the fact. 20 000 ms is chosen to sit just beyond the ~19 s
;  worst case the header computes, so the ladder can never be the
;  reason the deadman fires - if the run is going to overrun, it will
;  overrun on rung 0 for reasons that have nothing to do with this.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#JOIN_LADDER_MAX       = 4
#JOIN_LADDER_BUDGET_MS = 20000
#JOIN_5G_FIRST_CHANNEL = 15    ; channel 14 is the last 2.4 GHz one
#JOIN_RSSI_FLOOR  = -110       ; nothing real reads below this
#JOIN_RSSI_CEIL   = 0          ; nor above this

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HOW LONG TO LISTEN FOR EAPOL MESSAGE 1 AFTER THE LINK COMES UP.
;
;  An authenticator sends message 1 within a few tens of milliseconds
;  of the association response and retries it - hostapd's default is
;  four attempts a second apart (dot11RSNAConfigPairwiseUpdateCount).
;  2500 ms therefore covers the first frame and two retries, so a
;  single lost frame does not read as "the AP never spoke to us".
;
;  IT IS NOT LONGER BECAUSE OF THE DEADMAN. Everything above this point
;  has already spent most of fifteen seconds; a listen window that
;  causes the reset is a window whose result nobody sees.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#EAPOL_LISTEN_MS = 2500

; Our own six bytes, from the firmware rather than from the NVRAM text.
; A Global rather than a local because its address is taken.
Global Dim ourMac.a[6]


Procedure PutHexN(v.i, digits.i)
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  For i = digits - 1 To 0 Step -1
    n = (v >> (i * 4)) & $F
    If n < 10
      UartWrite(48 + n)
    Else
      UartWrite(55 + n)
    EndIf
  Next
EndProcedure

; PrintNl and PrintDec are uart.pi4's, at lines 1155 and 1119. Defining
; them again is a duplicate-procedure error rather than a shadow.
Procedure Line(*name, v.i)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  UartWriteStr(*name)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  PrintDec(v)
  PrintNl()
EndProcedure

Procedure HexLine(*name, v.i, digits.i)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  UartWriteStr(*name)
  UartWriteStr("  $")
  PutHexN(v, digits)
  PrintNl()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  UNTRUSTED TEXT FROM THE AIR, PRINTED SAFELY.
;
;  An SSID is 32 arbitrary bytes chosen by whoever runs the access
;  point. A hidden network's is all NULs; a hostile one can contain
;  escape sequences that move the cursor, clear the screen or rewrite
;  the lines above - which on a log that is going to be pasted into a
;  report is a way to make this program appear to say something it did
;  not. Byte by byte, everything outside printable ASCII replaced.
;
;  THE BOUND IS THE CALLER'S LENGTH AND ALSO 32. Both, because the two
;  can disagree: the length comes from the same untrusted frame.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure PrintSsid(p.i, n.i)
  Define j.i
  If n <= 0
    UartWriteStr("<hidden>")
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If n > 32
    n = 32
  EndIf
  For j = 0 To n - 1
    If PeekA(p + j) >= 32 And PeekA(p + j) <= 126
      UartWrite(PeekA(p + j))
    Else
      UartWrite(46)
    EndIf
  Next
EndProcedure

Procedure PrintBssid(p.i)
  Define j.i
  For j = 0 To 5
    PutHexN(PeekA(p + j) & $FF, 2)
    If j < 5
      UartWrite(58)
    EndIf
  Next
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE TRANSPORT INSTRUMENT. pi4WifiScan's, word for word, including the
;  UartDrain - which is the whole point of it. The announcement is made
;  BEFORE the dangerous call, so if that call never returns this line is
;  the last thing the board will ever say and it has to be out of the
;  PL011 before the call is attempted.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i probe_StepName(step.i)
  Select step
    Case 1
      ProcedureReturn "Cyw43WifiUp entered"
    Case 2
      ProcedureReturn "transport bookkeeping reset"
    Case 3
      ProcedureReturn "SENDING bsscfg:event_msgs"
    Case 4
      ProcedureReturn "event mask returned"
    Case 5
      ProcedureReturn "SENDING WLC_UP"
    Case 6
      ProcedureReturn "WLC_UP returned"
    Case 7
      ProcedureReturn "SENDING the escan request"
    Case 8
      ProcedureReturn "escan request returned"
    Case 9
      ProcedureReturn "no credit - draining to earn one"
    Case 10
      ProcedureReturn "pushing the request frame at F2"
    Case 11
      ProcedureReturn "WAITING for the reply"
    Case 12
      ProcedureReturn "the wait returned"
    Case 13
      ProcedureReturn "SENDING one clmload chunk"
    Case 14
      ProcedureReturn "every clmload chunk was accepted"
    Case 15
      ProcedureReturn "ASKING clmload_status"
    Case 16
      ProcedureReturn "clmload_status answered"
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn "unknown step - see THE TRACE SEAM in cyw43.pi4"
EndProcedure

Procedure probe_Trace(step.i)
  UartWriteStr("  .. ")
  PrintDec(step)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  UartWriteStr(probe_StepName(step))
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  crc32 over a DRAM buffer. pi4WifiScan's, unchanged, including the
;  run-time table build - the argument for which is in that file and is
;  about the offline gate rather than about the board.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CRC32_POLY = $EDB88320
Global Dim crcTab.l[256]
Global crcTabReady.i = 0

Procedure Crc32Init()
  Define i.i
  Define k.i
  Define c.i
  If crcTabReady = 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  For i = 0 To 255
    c = i
    For k = 0 To 7
      If (c & 1) = 1
        c = (c >> 1) ! #CRC32_POLY
      Else
        c = c >> 1
      EndIf
    Next
    crcTab[i] = c
  Next
  crcTabReady = 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Crc32(*p, n.i)
  Define c.i
  Define i.i
  Define idx.i
  Define tab.i
  Define q.i
  Crc32Init()
  tab = @crcTab[0]
  q = *p
  c = $FFFFFFFF
  For i = 1 To n
    idx = (c ! PeekA(q)) & $FF
    c = PeekN(tab + (idx << 2)) ! ((c >> 8) & $00FFFFFF)
    q = q + 1
  Next
  ProcedureReturn (~c) & $FFFFFFFF
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ELAPSED MILLISECONDS, so the deadman arithmetic in the header stops
;  being arithmetic. CNTPCT_EL0 and CNTFRQ_EL0 directly rather than
;  timer.pi4, because this payload already has four libraries in it and
;  a fifth for two readings is not worth the image.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i probe_Ticks()
  ASM
    mrs x0, cntpct_el0
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i probe_TickHz()
  ASM
    mrs x0, cntfrq_el0
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i NowMs()
  Define hz.i
  hz = probe_TickHz()
  If hz < 1000
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn probe_Ticks() / (hz / 1000)
EndProcedure

Global startMs.i = 0

Procedure Stamp(*what)
  UartWriteStr("  [t+")
  PrintDec(NowMs() - startMs)
  UartWriteStr(" ms] ")
  UartWriteStr(*what)
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  probe_RssiSane - is this reading a signed dBm figure at all?
;
;  THE CHECK EXISTS BECAUSE ITS FAILURE WOULD BE INVISIBLE. If a signed
;  field ever reached the table unsigned, -44 would arrive as 212 or as
;  65492, every 5 GHz candidate would clear a -70 floor, and the
;  fallback branch of the selection rule would never execute again -
;  with no error, no wrong number on the screen, and a rule that looks
;  like it works. So the rule refuses to run on a value it cannot
;  believe, and says so.
;
;  -110..0. Nothing real reads above 0 dBm, and -110 is below the noise
;  floor of any receiver in this class.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i probe_RssiSane(v.i)
  If v < #JOIN_RSSI_FLOOR Or v > #JOIN_RSSI_CEIL
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i probe_Is5GHz(chan.i)
  If chan >= #JOIN_5G_FIRST_CHANNEL
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  probe_Better - is candidate `a` a better pick than the incumbent `b`,
;  both already known to be in the same band and both stored?
;
;  Lowest stored slot, then strongest RSSI, then lowest BSSID. THE THIRD
;  TEST IS NOT PARANOIA: two access points broadcasting one SSID at the
;  same measured strength is an ordinary mesh, and without it the winner
;  would be whichever the scan happened to report first - which is the
;  one thing the rule is required not to be.
;
;  b = -1 means there is no incumbent yet.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i probe_Better(a.i, slotA.i, b.i, slotB.i)
  Define j.i
  Define x.i
  Define y.i

  If b < 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  If slotA < slotB
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  If slotA > slotB
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If Cyw43ScanRssi(a) > Cyw43ScanRssi(b)
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  If Cyw43ScanRssi(a) < Cyw43ScanRssi(b)
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  For j = 0 To 5
    x = Cyw43ScanBssidByte(a, j)
    y = Cyw43ScanBssidByte(b, j)
    If x < y
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf
    If x > y
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
  Next
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure



; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE LADDER, BUILT BY APPLYING THE ONE RULE REPEATEDLY.
;
;  ladderRow[k] is the scan row of the k-th candidate to try and
;  ladderSlot[k] is the settings slot it came from. probe_BuildLadder
;  returns how many rungs it filled.
;
;  IT IS THE SAME RULE, NOT A SECOND ONE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN.
;  The three-tier preference - a 5 GHz candidate at or above the floor,
;  else any 2.4 GHz candidate, else a below-floor 5 GHz one, ties broken
;  by slot then RSSI then BSSID by probe_Better - is run once per rung
;  over the candidates not yet taken. So rung 0 is exactly what the
;  single-choice code chose, and rung k is exactly what it would have
;  chosen if the first k networks had not existed. A separate "retry
;  rule" would have been a second thing to keep in step with the first,
;  and the first is the one this file documents at length.
;
;  ladderTaken IS THE ONLY MUTABLE STATE and it marks rows already
;  placed on the ladder. It is sized to #CYW43_MAX_BSS because that is
;  the size of the scan table it indexes; a smaller array with a bound
;  check would be the same thing with a way to get the bound wrong.
;
;  A ROW THAT IS NOT STORED NEVER ENTERS. SettingsWifiFindSsid returning
;  0 means no credential, and a candidate with no credential is not a
;  candidate - it is a network somebody else owns.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Global Dim ladderRow.i[#JOIN_LADDER_MAX]
Global Dim ladderSlot.i[#JOIN_LADDER_MAX]
Global Dim ladderTaken.a[#CYW43_MAX_BSS]

Procedure.i probe_BuildLadder(found.i)
  Define i.i
  Define k.i
  Define slot.i
  Define rssi.i
  Define chan.i
  Define best5.i
  Define best5Slot.i
  Define best24.i
  Define best24Slot.i
  Define weak5.i
  Define weak5Slot.i
  Define pick.i
  Define pickSlot.i

  For i = 0 To #CYW43_MAX_BSS - 1
    ladderTaken[i] = 0
  Next

  k = 0
  While k < #JOIN_LADDER_MAX
    best5 = -1
    best5Slot = 0
    best24 = -1
    best24Slot = 0
    weak5 = -1
    weak5Slot = 0

    For i = 0 To found - 1
      If ladderTaken[i] = 0
        slot = SettingsWifiFindSsid(Cyw43ScanSsid(i))
        If slot <> 0
          rssi = Cyw43ScanRssi(i)
          chan = Cyw43ScanChannel(i)
          If probe_Is5GHz(chan) = 1
            If rssi >= #JOIN_5G_FLOOR_DBM
              If probe_Better(i, slot, best5, best5Slot) = 1
                best5 = i
                best5Slot = slot
              EndIf
            Else
              If probe_Better(i, slot, weak5, weak5Slot) = 1
                weak5 = i
                weak5Slot = slot
              EndIf
            EndIf
          Else
            If probe_Better(i, slot, best24, best24Slot) = 1
              best24 = i
              best24Slot = slot
            EndIf
          EndIf
        EndIf
      EndIf
    Next

    ; The rule, applied. Three lines, in this order, and the order IS
    ; the rule - the same three lines the single-choice code ran, kept
    ; here in the same order for the same reasons stated there.
    pick = -1
    pickSlot = 0
    If best5 >= 0
      pick = best5
      pickSlot = best5Slot
    ElseIf best24 >= 0
      pick = best24
      pickSlot = best24Slot
    ElseIf weak5 >= 0
      pick = weak5
      pickSlot = weak5Slot
    EndIf

    If pick < 0
      ProcedureReturn k
    EndIf

    ladderRow[k] = pick
    ladderSlot[k] = pickSlot
    ladderTaken[pick] = 1
    k = k + 1
  Wend

  ProcedureReturn k
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  probe_FallThrough - may the ladder move to the next rung after this
;  failure?
;
;  ONLY FOR A REFUSAL THAT A DIFFERENT NETWORK COULD FIX, and the list
;  is short on purpose:
;
;    JOINFAIL_ASSOC  the association was refused. On this bench that is
;                    802.11 status 43 AKMP_NOT_VALID from the 5 GHz
;                    radio, and it is THE case this ladder was written
;                    for. It arrives in milliseconds, so falling
;                    through costs almost nothing.
;    JOINFAIL_PRUNE  RSN mismatch. cyw43.pi4's own text says RETRYING
;                    UNCHANGED WILL LOOP FOR EVER - which is an argument
;                    for trying a DIFFERENT network and against trying
;                    the same one, so it belongs here and nowhere else.
;    JOINFAIL_NONET  the SSID was not on the air. Same reasoning: this
;                    network cannot be joined now, another might.
;
;  AND FOR NOTHING ELSE. The three that are deliberately excluded:
;
;    JOINFAIL_WRONGPASS   the access point's own verdict on the key. A
;                         stored credential is wrong and that is a
;                         configuration fault worth stopping on; walking
;                         quietly to the next network would hide it.
;    JOINFAIL_KEYTIMEOUT  the fix for this one is a disassociate and a
;                         retry of the SAME network - measured on the
;                         twin at 21 of 22 first attempts - and it is a
;                         different mechanism from this ladder. Falling
;                         through would take the one network that was
;                         about to work and abandon it.
;    JOINFAIL_TIMEOUT     slow by definition, and it leaves the radio in
;                         a state nothing here has characterised. Two of
;                         those would eat the whole deadman budget.
;
;  BADAUTH and LINKLOST are excluded by omission, for the timeout
;  reason: neither is a fast, unambiguous "this network said no".
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i probe_FallThrough(failCode.i)
  If failCode = #CYW43_JOINFAIL_ASSOC
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  If failCode = #CYW43_JOINFAIL_PRUNE
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  If failCode = #CYW43_JOINFAIL_NONET
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE CRYPTO, ON SILICON, AGAINST PUBLISHED VECTORS
; ======================================================================
;
;  WHY THIS RUNS FIRST, BEFORE A SINGLE RADIO REGISTER IS TOUCHED. The
;  host now has to be the WPA2 supplicant, because this firmware has
;  none - finding 3 above, four independent refusals. A supplicant that
;  computes the wrong PMK produces a join that fails with no diagnosis
;  available anywhere: the access point simply stops answering, and
;  every symptom points at the radio. So the arithmetic is proven
;  BEFORE the radio is powered, and if it is wrong this file says so
;  and stops rather than spending twenty seconds blaming the air.
;
;  IT COSTS ABOUT A TENTH OF A SECOND. Nine vectors, of which two are
;  4096-iteration derivations - roughly 16,000 SHA-1 compressions each.
;  On a 600 MHz core that is tens of milliseconds apiece. It fits inside
;  the deadman budget at the top of this file with room to spare, and
;  the elapsed-ms row after it turns this estimate into a measurement.
;
;  EVERY VALUE HERE IS PUBLISHED. Two SHA-1 digests from FIPS PUB 180-1
;  by way of RFC 3174 section 7.3; three HMAC-SHA1 cases from RFC 2202
;  section 3, one of them the 96-bit truncation; three PBKDF2 vectors
;  from RFC 6070; and the passphrase-to-PSK example from IEEE 802.11i
;  Annex H.4.2, which is the passphrase "password" against the SSID
;  "IEEE".
;
;  *** THERE IS NO REAL CREDENTIAL IN THIS SECTION AND THERE MUST NEVER
;  BE ONE. *** The published vectors exist precisely so that nobody has
;  to test with a live passphrase. The real one is read from
;  SETTINGS.TXT further down, is never printed, and appears in no source
;  file. If a future edit is tempted to "just check it against the real
;  network's PMK", the answer is no: a PMK in a console log is the
;  passphrase for every practical purpose, and this log gets pasted into
;  reports.
;
;  THE SAME VECTORS ARE THE OFFLINE GATE'S. tools/a64/a64_sha1_check.py,
;  a64_hmacsha1_check.py and a64_pbkdf2_check.py run these and several
;  hundred more against a model of the instruction set. This section is
;  the part that runs on the PART - the model has no caches and no real
;  memory system, so agreement between the two is worth having and
;  disagreement would be worth a great deal more.
; ======================================================================

Global Dim cryptoOut.a[40]         ; digest / MAC / derived key workspace
Global Dim cryptoKey.a[24]         ; a constructed HMAC key

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  crypto_Expect - compare `words` big-endian 32-bit words at `got`
;  against the Data.l table at `want`.
;
;  THE TABLE IS WORDS AND THE BUFFER IS BYTES, so the comparison
;  reassembles four bytes into a word rather than casting either side.
;  A digest is defined as a byte string; storing the expectation as
;  words is only a way to write it compactly, and the reassembly is
;  where the two representations are reconciled explicitly instead of
;  by an endianness assumption nobody checked.
;
;  PeekN, NOT PeekL, on the table: half the words in a digest have bit
;  31 set and PeekL sign-extends on this target. PeekA, not PeekB, on
;  the buffer, for the same reason one octet down.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i crypto_Expect(got.i, want.i, words.i)
  Define w.i
  Define v.i
  Define g.i
  For w = 0 To words - 1
    v = PeekN(want + w * 4) & $FFFFFFFF
    g = ((PeekA(got + w * 4) & 255) << 24) | ((PeekA(got + w * 4 + 1) & 255) << 16) | ((PeekA(got + w * 4 + 2) & 255) << 8) | (PeekA(got + w * 4 + 3) & 255)
    If g <> v
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
  Next
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure crypto_Row(*name, ok.i)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  UartWriteStr(*name)
  If ok = 1
    UartWriteStr("  ok")
  Else
    UartWriteStr("  *** WRONG ***")
  EndIf
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The vector inputs, returned from Procedure.i so they arrive as
;  addresses. That spelling is this file's own - probe_StepName above
;  does the same thing - and it is used here because the alternative, a
;  byte table, would hide plain ASCII behind hex for no gain.
;
;  THEIR LENGTHS ARE WRITTEN OUT AT EACH CALL SITE rather than measured,
;  because the document states a length and that length is part of the
;  vector. RFC 2202 case 2's data is 28 octets; if a future edit changed
;  the text and left the 28, the vector would fail, which is correct.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i crypto_Abc()
  ProcedureReturn "abc"
EndProcedure

Procedure.i crypto_Abcd56()
  ProcedureReturn "abcdbcdecdefdefgefghfghighijhijkijkljklmklmnlmnomnopnopq"
EndProcedure

Procedure.i crypto_HiThere()
  ProcedureReturn "Hi There"
EndProcedure

Procedure.i crypto_Jefe()
  ProcedureReturn "Jefe"
EndProcedure

Procedure.i crypto_WhatDoYa()
  ProcedureReturn "what do ya want for nothing?"
EndProcedure

Procedure.i crypto_TestWithTruncation()
  ProcedureReturn "Test With Truncation"
EndProcedure

Procedure.i crypto_Password()
  ProcedureReturn "password"
EndProcedure

Procedure.i crypto_Salt()
  ProcedureReturn "salt"
EndProcedure

Procedure.i crypto_Ieee()
  ProcedureReturn "IEEE"
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  CryptoSelfTest - returns the number of vectors that came out wrong.
;  Zero is the only acceptable answer.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i CryptoSelfTest()
  Define bad.i
  Define ok.i
  Define i.i
  Define n.i

  bad = 0

  ; ---- SHA-1, RFC 3174 section 7.3 / FIPS PUB 180-1 ------------------
  Sha1Of(crypto_Abc(), 3, @cryptoOut[0])
  ok = crypto_Expect(@cryptoOut[0], ?vecSha1Abc, 5)
  crypto_Row("sha1   RFC 3174 TEST1 ", ok)
  bad = bad + (1 - ok)

  Sha1Of(crypto_Abcd56(), 56, @cryptoOut[0])
  ok = crypto_Expect(@cryptoOut[0], ?vecSha1Abcd, 5)
  crypto_Row("sha1   RFC 3174 TEST2 ", ok)
  bad = bad + (1 - ok)

  ; ---- HMAC-SHA1, RFC 2202 section 3 ---------------------------------
  ; Case 1: a 20-byte key of $0B. Built in a loop rather than written
  ; out, because twenty identical bytes typed by hand is nineteen
  ; chances to be wrong and no chance to be interesting.
  For i = 0 To 19
    cryptoKey[i] = $0B
  Next
  HmacSha1Of(@cryptoKey[0], 20, crypto_HiThere(), 8, @cryptoOut[0])
  ok = crypto_Expect(@cryptoOut[0], ?vecHmacCase1, 5)
  crypto_Row("hmac   RFC 2202 case 1", ok)
  bad = bad + (1 - ok)

  ; Case 2: the key is ASCII and shorter than the block.
  HmacSha1Of(crypto_Jefe(), 4, crypto_WhatDoYa(), 28, @cryptoOut[0])
  ok = crypto_Expect(@cryptoOut[0], ?vecHmacCase2, 5)
  crypto_Row("hmac   RFC 2202 case 2", ok)
  bad = bad + (1 - ok)

  ; Case 5, TRUNCATED TO 96 BITS. The RFC publishes the short answer as
  ; well as the long one, and this is the truncation path the EAPOL-Key
  ; MIC will use at 128 bits - so it is checked here rather than taken
  ; on trust from the full-length cases. The RETURNED LENGTH is checked
  ; too: a truncation that writes the right twelve bytes and reports
  ; twenty would break the caller and not this comparison.
  For i = 0 To 19
    cryptoKey[i] = $0C
  Next
  HmacSha1Key(@cryptoKey[0], 20)
  HmacSha1Update(crypto_TestWithTruncation(), 20)
  n = HmacSha1End(@cryptoOut[0], 12)
  ok = crypto_Expect(@cryptoOut[0], ?vecHmacCase5t, 3)
  If n <> 12
    ok = 0
  EndIf
  crypto_Row("hmac   RFC 2202 case 5, cut to 96 bits", ok)
  bad = bad + (1 - ok)

  ; ---- PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA1, RFC 6070 ------------------------------------
  ; c = 1 and c = 2 are the two that can tell an off-by-one in the
  ; iteration loop from a correct one. c = 4096 is the one that costs
  ; what a real derivation costs, and is here to prove the cost as much
  ; as the answer.
  n = Pbkdf2Sha1(crypto_Password(), 8, crypto_Salt(), 4, 1, @cryptoOut[0], 20)
  ok = crypto_Expect(@cryptoOut[0], ?vecPbkdf2C1, 5)
  If n <> 20
    ok = 0
  EndIf
  crypto_Row("pbkdf2 RFC 6070 c=1   ", ok)
  bad = bad + (1 - ok)

  n = Pbkdf2Sha1(crypto_Password(), 8, crypto_Salt(), 4, 2, @cryptoOut[0], 20)
  ok = crypto_Expect(@cryptoOut[0], ?vecPbkdf2C2, 5)
  If n <> 20
    ok = 0
  EndIf
  crypto_Row("pbkdf2 RFC 6070 c=2   ", ok)
  bad = bad + (1 - ok)

  n = Pbkdf2Sha1(crypto_Password(), 8, crypto_Salt(), 4, 4096, @cryptoOut[0], 20)
  ok = crypto_Expect(@cryptoOut[0], ?vecPbkdf2C4096, 5)
  If n <> 20
    ok = 0
  EndIf
  crypto_Row("pbkdf2 RFC 6070 c=4096", ok)
  bad = bad + (1 - ok)

  ; ---- the WPA2 mapping, IEEE 802.11i Annex H.4.2 --------------------
  ; PSK = PBKDF2(passphrase, SSID, 4096, 32). This is the exact call
  ; a join will make with the real credential, run here on the
  ; standard's own worked example.
  n = Wpa2Psk(crypto_Password(), 8, crypto_Ieee(), 4, @cryptoOut[0])
  ok = crypto_Expect(@cryptoOut[0], ?vecPskIeee, 8)
  If n <> 32
    ok = 0
  EndIf
  crypto_Row("psk    IEEE Annex H.4 ", ok)
  bad = bad + (1 - ok)

  ; The workspace held a PUBLISHED PMK, not a real one, but wiping it
  ; anyway means the habit lives in the file rather than in somebody's
  ; memory.
  For i = 0 To 39
    cryptoOut[i] = 0
  Next
  Pbkdf2Wipe()

  ProcedureReturn bad
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE PUBLISHED EXPECTATIONS, as big-endian 32-bit words.
;
;  Data.l because each entry must occupy four bytes and be reachable at
;  base + w * 4; read with PeekN, never PeekL, because half of these
;  words have bit 31 set. Data has no unsigned spelling and does not
;  need one - what is being asked of it is a four-byte slot, not a sign.
;
;  EVERY ONE OF THESE IS FROM A PUBLISHED DOCUMENT and NOT ONE was
;  produced by running this tree. The same values are parsed straight
;  out of those documents by tools/a64/a64_sha1_check.py,
;  a64_hmacsha1_check.py and a64_pbkdf2_check.py, so a typo below
;  disagrees with the offline gate rather than quietly redefining the
;  answer.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
DataSection
  ; SHA-1("abc") - RFC 3174 section 7.3, resultarray[0]
  vecSha1Abc:
  Data.l $A9993E36, $4706816A, $BA3E2571, $7850C26C, $9CD0D89D

  ; SHA-1 of the 56-byte two-block message - resultarray[1]
  vecSha1Abcd:
  Data.l $84983E44, $1C3BD26E, $BAAE4AA1, $F95129E5, $E54670F1

  ; HMAC-SHA1, RFC 2202 section 3, case 1
  vecHmacCase1:
  Data.l $B6173186, $55057264, $E28BC0B6, $FB378C8E, $F146BE00

  ; case 2
  vecHmacCase2:
  Data.l $EFFCDF6A, $E5EB2FA2, $D27416D5, $F184DF9C, $259A7C79

  ; case 5, the published digest-96
  vecHmacCase5t:
  Data.l $4C1A0342, $4B55E07F, $E7F27BE1

  ; PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA1 - RFC 6070
  vecPbkdf2C1:
  Data.l $0C60C80F, $961F0E71, $F3A9B524, $AF601206, $2FE037A6

  vecPbkdf2C2:
  Data.l $EA6C014D, $C72D6F8C, $CD1ED92A, $CE1D41F0, $D8DE8957

  vecPbkdf2C4096:
  Data.l $4B007901, $B765489A, $BEAD49D9, $26F721D0, $65A429C1

  ; The PSK for passphrase "password" and SSID "IEEE" - IEEE 802.11i
  ; Annex H.4.2. The CONSTRUCTION is cited on disk at
  ; RaspberryPi4/Reference/hostap_sha1-pbkdf2.c:71-72; the standard
  ; itself is not freely redistributable and is not on this disk, so
  ; tools/a64/a64_pbkdf2_check.py confirms this value against an
  ; implementation outside this tree before it uses it.
  vecPskIeee:
  Data.l $F42C6FC5, $2DF0EBEF, $9EBB4B90, $B38A5F90
  Data.l $2E83FE1B, $135A70E2, $3AED762E, $9710A12E
EndDataSection


Procedure.i Main()
  Define ok.i
  Define hz.i
  Define i.i
  Define j.i
  Define r.i
  Define crc.i
  Define n.i
  Define slices.i
  Define stop.i
  Define done.i
  Define found.i
  Define setLen.i
  Define slot.i
  Define chosen.i
  Define chosenSlot.i
  Define best5.i
  Define best5Slot.i
  Define best24.i
  Define best24Slot.i
  Define weak5.i
  Define weak5Slot.i
  Define rssi.i
  Define chan.i
  Define pass.i
  Define passLen.i
  Define ssid.i
  Define ssidLen.i
  Define verdict.i
  Define matched.i
  Define bssidOk.i
  Define supGone.i
  Define pmkOk.i
  Define rungs.i
  Define rung.i
  Define attempts.i
  Define fellThrough.i
  Define k.i
  Define p.i
  Define eapolLen.i
  Define eapolMs.i

  startMs = NowMs()

  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("=== pi4 CYW43455 join ===")
  PrintNl()
  PrintNl()

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 0 - THE CRYPTO NO LONGER RUNS HERE, AND THE REASON IS A MEASUREMENT.
  ;
  ; The block above CryptoSelfTest estimated "about a tenth of a
  ; second ... tens of milliseconds apiece" for the two 4096-iteration
  ; derivations. MEASURED ON SILICON, 2026-08-28, COM7, deadman 15:
  ;
  ;     -- the supplicant crypto, published vectors --
  ;       ... all nine vectors ok ...
  ;       elapsed ms         15497
  ;
  ; FIFTEEN AND A HALF SECONDS, and the deadman fired on the next line
  ; with the radio still cold. The estimate was out by a factor of
  ; about 150, in the one direction that costs the entire run.
  ;
  ; WHY, said plainly so nobody re-derives it: the MMU is off, so every
  ; instruction fetch and every load is Device memory with no cache
  ; anywhere. 4096 iterations over two output blocks is 16,384 HMAC-
  ; SHA1 calls, and at roughly 1.2 ms each that is ten seconds for ONE
  ; passphrase-to-PSK mapping. It is NOT a defect in sha1.pi4 or
  ; pbkdf2.pi4 - every published vector passed, on the board, which is
  ; what that run proved before it ran out of clock.
  ;
  ; SO THE ARITHMETIC IS PROVEN IN ITS OWN PAYLOAD AND THE PMK IS
  ; DERIVED IN ANOTHER (pi4WifiPmk.pi4, which stages it in DRAM). This
  ; file's fifteen seconds are for the radio. CryptoSelfTest and its
  ; vectors are left below, uncalled: deleting them would delete the
  ; citation for every value in them.
  ; ====================================================================

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 1 - what is in DRAM
  ; ====================================================================
  UartWriteStr("-- the blobs, as loaded --")
  PrintNl()

  crc = Crc32(#FW_ADDR, #FW_LEN)
  HexLine("firmware crc32   ", crc, 8)
  If crc <> #FW_CRC
    HexLine("  EXPECTED       ", #FW_CRC, 8)
    UartWriteStr("  WRONG - type:  load BRCMFW.BIN 1000000")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at crc ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  crc = Crc32(#NV_ADDR, #NV_LEN)
  HexLine("nvram crc32      ", crc, 8)
  If crc <> #NV_CRC
    HexLine("  EXPECTED       ", #NV_CRC, 8)
    UartWriteStr("  WRONG - type:  load BRCMNV.TXT 1200000")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at crc ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  crc = Crc32(#CLM_ADDR, #CLM_LEN)
  HexLine("clm crc32        ", crc, 8)
  If crc <> #CLM_CRC
    HexLine("  EXPECTED       ", #CLM_CRC, 8)
    UartWriteStr("  WRONG - type:  load BRCMCLM.BLB 1300000")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at crc ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 2 - THE CREDENTIALS, off the stick and into the settings table.
  ;
  ; SettingsParse TOUCHES NO MEDIUM. It is a pass over a byte buffer -
  ; settings.pi4 says so in its header and it is what makes the whole
  ; library testable offline - so this payload never brings USB up and
  ; never competes with Anvil for it. Anvil did the reading; this only
  ; parses what Anvil left in DRAM.
  ; ====================================================================
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("-- the stored networks --")
  PrintNl()

  ; Find the end by scanning for the NUL the `fill` left behind. A
  ; REFUSAL RATHER THAN A GUESS if there is not one inside the window:
  ; parsing eight kilobytes of unknown DRAM as a settings file would
  ; report a syntax error on a line that is not in the file, which sends
  ; somebody to edit a file that is perfectly correct.
  setLen = -1
  For i = 0 To #SET_WIN - 1
    If PeekA(#SET_ADDR + i) = 0
      setLen = i
      Break
    EndIf
  Next
  If setLen < 0
    UartWriteStr("  NO TERMINATOR in 8 KB at $01400000. Either the fill was")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  skipped or SETTINGS.TXT is larger than this window. Type:")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("    fill 1400000 0 800")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("    load SETTINGS.TXT 1400000")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at the settings ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  Line("settings bytes   ", setLen)
  If setLen = 0
    UartWriteStr("  SETTINGS.TXT IS EMPTY, or the load did not happen. There")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  are no credentials to join with.")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at the settings ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ok = SettingsParse(#SET_ADDR, setLen)
  Line("SettingsParse (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(SettingsErrorText())
    PrintNl()
    Line("  at file line   ", SettingsErrorLine())
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at the settings ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  Line("keys in the table", SettingsCount())
  Line("duplicate keys   ", SettingsDuplicates())

  ; THE STORED LIST, IN PREFERENCE ORDER, WITH NO PASSPHRASES ON IT.
  ; The slot number and the SSID are what the selection is decided on
  ; and are therefore what has to be printed; the passphrase's LENGTH is
  ; printed beside it because "the slot is there but its passphrase is
  ; 0 bytes" is a real and otherwise silent failure.
  ;
  ; WALKED WITH THE CURSOR, NOT WITH `For slot = 1 To
  ; SettingsWifiSlots()`. The obvious loop misses the LEGACY pseudo-slot
  ; - settings.pi4 keeps the old flat wifi.network / wifi.password pair
  ; readable as slot #SET_WIFI_LEGACY, one past the numbered ones, so a
  ; stick written by yesterday's build still joins. A bound of
  ; SettingsWifiSlots() would silently exclude exactly the credential
  ; the backward-compatibility promise was made about.
  n = 0
  slot = SettingsWifiNextSlot(0)
  While slot <> 0
    n = n + 1
    UartWriteStr("  slot ")
    PrintDec(slot)
    If slot = SettingsWifiLegacySlot()
      UartWriteStr(" (old flat keys)")
    Else
      UartWriteStr("                ")
    EndIf
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    ssid = SettingsWifiSlotSsid(slot)
    PrintSsid(ssid, SettingsLength(SettingsWifiSlotSsidKey(slot)))
    UartWriteStr("   passphrase bytes ")
    PrintDec(SettingsLength(SettingsWifiSlotPasswordKey(slot)))
    PrintNl()
    slot = SettingsWifiNextSlot(slot)
  Wend
  Line("stored networks  ", n)
  If n = 0
    UartWriteStr("  NOTHING IS STORED. Type, at the prompt:")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("    wifi ssid 1 <name>")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("    wifi password 1 <passphrase>")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("    settings save")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at the settings ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 3 - the bus. The mailbox preamble is not optional.
  ; ====================================================================
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("-- bus --")
  PrintNl()

  ok = MailboxInit()
  Line("mailbox init (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at the mailbox ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  hz = MailboxClockRate(#CLOCK_ID_EMMC)
  If hz <= 0
    UartWriteStr("  the firmware would not report the EMMC clock.")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at the clock ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  SdioSetBaseClockKhz(hz / 1000)

  ok = SdioAssertWlOn()
  Line("WL_ON asserted (want 1)", ok)

  ok = SdioInit()
  Line("SdioInit (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(SdioErrorText())
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at the bus ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ok = SdioEnableFunction(1)
  Line("enable F1 (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(SdioErrorText())
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at function 1 ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 4 - the chip and the firmware
  ; ====================================================================
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("-- chip and firmware --")
  PrintNl()

  ok = Cyw43SetIo(@SdioCmd52Read, @SdioCmd52Write, @SdioCmd53Read, @SdioCmd53Write)
  Line("Cyw43SetIo (want 1)", ok)
  ok = Cyw43SetBlockIo(@SdioCmd53ReadBlocks, @SdioCmd53WriteBlocks)
  Line("Cyw43SetBlockIo (want 1)", ok)

  ok = Cyw43Attach()
  Line("Cyw43Attach (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(Cyw43ErrorText())
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at the chip ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  HexLine("chip id          ", Cyw43ChipId(), 4)

  ok = Cyw43SetBlockMode(1)
  Line("block mode on (want 1)", ok)

  ok = Cyw43PrepareDownload()
  Line("Cyw43PrepareDownload (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(Cyw43ErrorText())
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped before the download ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ok = Cyw43UploadFirmware(#FW_ADDR, #FW_LEN, #SCRATCH, #SCRATCH_LEN)
  Line("Cyw43UploadFirmware (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(Cyw43ErrorText())
    PrintNl()
    HexLine("  first bad addr ", Cyw43BadAddr(), 8)
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at the image ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ok = Cyw43UploadNvram(#NV_ADDR, #NV_LEN, #NV_WORK, #NV_WORK_MAX, #SCRATCH, #SCRATCH_LEN)
  Line("Cyw43UploadNvram (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(Cyw43ErrorText())
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at the nvram ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ok = Cyw43Start()
  Line("Cyw43Start (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(Cyw43ErrorText())
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped releasing the ARM ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ok = Cyw43HtClock()
  Line("Cyw43HtClock (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(Cyw43ErrorText())
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at the HT clock ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ok = Cyw43AnnounceHost()
  Line("Cyw43AnnounceHost (want 1)", ok)

  ok = SdioEnableFunction(2)
  Line("enable F2 (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  F2 NEVER CAME READY - the firmware did not boot.")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at F2 ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  Stamp("firmware up")

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 5 - the transport, with THE JOIN EVENT MASK
  ; ====================================================================
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("-- transport --")
  PrintNl()

  Cyw43SetTrace(@probe_Trace, 0)

  ; #CYW43_EVMASK_JOIN, which is the one difference from pi4WifiScan's
  ; call. It adds the ten event types an association is decided by; see
  ; the argument at Cyw43SetEventMask for why it is an argument and not
  ; simply always on.
  r = Cyw43WifiUp(#CLM_ADDR, #CLM_LEN, #CYW43_EVMASK_JOIN, 2000)
  Line("Cyw43WifiUp (want 0)", r)
  Line("  clm chunks sent", Cyw43ClmChunks())
  Line("  clm status     ", Cyw43ClmStatus())
  If r <> 0
    Line("  ioctl status   ", Cyw43IoctlStatus())
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at wifi up ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  Stamp("radio up")

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 5b - THE BASELINE. Ask whether we are associated BEFORE joining.
  ;
  ; WITHOUT THIS THE READ-BACK AFTER THE JOIN PROVES NOTHING. A firmware
  ; that answered "associated" to everything would look identical to one
  ; that had just associated, and there would be no way to tell from the
  ; log. -17 BCME_NOTASSOCIATED here is what makes the second reading
  ; evidence. It is also the first time this driver has ever sent
  ; WLC_GET_BSSID, so whether -17 really comes back is itself unverified
  ; and this row is the verification.
  ; ====================================================================
  r = Cyw43ReadBssid(2000)
  Line("baseline associated (want 0)", r)
  Line("  raw ioctl status (want -17)", Cyw43IoctlStatus())
  If r = 1
    UartWriteStr("  ALREADY ASSOCIATED before we joined. The radio was left")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  joined by a previous run; the proof below cannot")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  distinguish that link from one this run made.")
    PrintNl()
  EndIf

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 6 - the scan
  ; ====================================================================
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("-- scan --")
  PrintNl()

  r = Cyw43ScanStart(0, 2000)
  Line("Cyw43ScanStart (want 0)", r)
  If r <> 0
    Line("  ioctl status   ", Cyw43IoctlStatus())
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped starting the scan ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  done = -1
  slices = 0
  stop = 0
  While stop = 0 And slices < (#SCAN_BUDGET_MS / #SCAN_SLICE_MS)
    r = Cyw43ScanPoll(#SCAN_SLICE_MS)
    slices = slices + 1
    If r = #CYW43_SCAN_DONE
      done = 0
      stop = 1
    EndIf
    If r = #CYW43_SCAN_FAILED
      done = 1
      stop = 1
    EndIf
  Wend
  Line("slices used      ", slices)
  If done = -1
    UartWriteStr("  NO COMPLETION EVENT within the budget - which is NOT the")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  same as a scan that found nothing.")
    PrintNl()
  ElseIf done = 1
    UartWriteStr("  scan completed, status ")
    PrintDec(Cyw43EventStatus())
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
  Stamp("scan done")

  found = Cyw43ScanCount()
  Line("networks found   ", found)

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 7 - THE SELECTION. See the rule at the head of this file.
  ; ====================================================================
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("-- selecting --")
  PrintNl()

  For i = 0 To found - 1
    ssid = Cyw43ScanSsid(i)
    ssidLen = Cyw43ScanSsidLen(i)
    rssi = Cyw43ScanRssi(i)
    chan = Cyw43ScanChannel(i)

    UartWriteStr("  ")
    PrintBssid(@cyw43_bssBssid[0] + i * 6)
    UartWriteStr("  ch ")
    PrintDec(chan)
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    PrintDec(rssi)
    UartWriteStr(" dBm  ")
    PrintSsid(ssid, ssidLen)

    ; THE SIGN CHECK, PER ROW AND NOT ONCE. A table where one entry
    ; survived sign extension and another did not is a stranger fault
    ; than one where none did, and it is the one a single check would
    ; miss.
    If probe_RssiSane(rssi) = 0
      UartWriteStr("   <- RSSI IS NOT A SIGNED dBm READING. Refusing.")
      PrintNl()
      UartWriteStr("=== stopped at the selection ===")
      PrintNl()
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf

    ; IS IT STORED? The comparison is settings.pi4's, byte for byte,
    ; because two of the real networks differ from other saved profiles
    ; by a single letter and one differs only by a suffix.
    slot = SettingsWifiFindSsid(ssid)
    If slot = 0
      UartWriteStr("   - not stored, skipped")
      PrintNl()
    Else
      UartWriteStr("   <- stored in slot ")
      PrintDec(slot)
      ; NARRATION ONLY. Which tier each row lands in is printed here
      ; and DECIDED in probe_BuildLadder, which runs the same three
      ; tests over the same values. Two places, one rule: the tier
      ; names below must keep agreeing with the tests there, and they
      ; are side by side in this file so that a change to one that
      ; misses the other is visible in a diff.
      If probe_Is5GHz(chan) = 1
        If rssi >= #JOIN_5G_FLOOR_DBM
          UartWriteStr(", 5 GHz, above the floor")
        Else
          UartWriteStr(", 5 GHz, BELOW the floor")
        EndIf
      Else
        UartWriteStr(", 2.4 GHz")
      EndIf
      PrintNl()
    EndIf
  Next

  Line("5 GHz floor dBm  ", #JOIN_5G_FLOOR_DBM)

  ; THE RULE, APPLIED - REPEATEDLY, TO BUILD A LADDER.
  ;
  ; The rule itself is unchanged and lives in probe_BuildLadder: a
  ; healthy 5 GHz link beats everything; failing that, any 2.4 GHz
  ; link; failing that, a weak 5 GHz link, because a marginal link is
  ; better than no link and refusing here would turn "prefer 5 GHz
  ; unless it is weak" into "refuse to associate when 5 GHz is weak and
  ; there is no 2.4 GHz", which nobody asked for.
  ;
  ; WHAT CHANGED ON 2026-08-28 IS THAT IT IS APPLIED MORE THAN ONCE.
  ; Rung 0 is the choice the single-choice code made, unchanged; each
  ; later rung is what that code would have chosen with the earlier
  ; rungs deleted from the scan. The join below walks the ladder and
  ; falls through ONLY on a failure a different network could fix -
  ; see probe_FallThrough, which is deliberately short.
  rungs = probe_BuildLadder(found)
  Line("candidates on the ladder", rungs)

  If rungs < 1
    UartWriteStr("  NONE OF THE VISIBLE NETWORKS IS STORED, and none of the")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  stored ones is visible. THAT IS NOT A FAULT IN THIS CODE -")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  a network that is not on the air cannot be joined. The scan")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  list above and the stored list further up are the report.")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped: nothing to join ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ; THE WHOLE LADDER IS PRINTED BEFORE ANY OF IT IS TRIED, so the log
  ; records the plan and not only what happened to it. A run that
  ; succeeds on rung 0 still shows what it would have fallen through
  ; to, which is the difference between "the rule worked" and "the rule
  ; had one option and got lucky".
  For i = 0 To rungs - 1
    UartWriteStr("  rung ")
    PrintDec(i)
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    PrintBssid(@cyw43_bssBssid[0] + ladderRow[i] * 6)
    UartWriteStr("  ch ")
    PrintDec(Cyw43ScanChannel(ladderRow[i]))
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    PrintDec(Cyw43ScanRssi(ladderRow[i]))
    UartWriteStr(" dBm  slot ")
    PrintDec(ladderSlot[i])
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    PrintSsid(Cyw43ScanSsid(ladderRow[i]), Cyw43ScanSsidLen(ladderRow[i]))
    PrintNl()
  Next

  chosen = ladderRow[0]
  chosenSlot = ladderSlot[0]

  ; SELECTED BY BSSID. The row is identified by its six bytes from here
  ; on, and those same six bytes are what the firmware is asked to
  ; confirm after the join. The name is printed too, for a reader.
  UartWriteStr("  CHOSEN  ")
  PrintBssid(@cyw43_bssBssid[0] + chosen * 6)
  UartWriteStr("  ch ")
  PrintDec(Cyw43ScanChannel(chosen))
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  PrintDec(Cyw43ScanRssi(chosen))
  UartWriteStr(" dBm  ")
  PrintSsid(Cyw43ScanSsid(chosen), Cyw43ScanSsidLen(chosen))
  PrintNl()

  ; THE SELECTION TEST IS ON THE ENTRY, NOT ON THE PASSPHRASE. Every
  ; stored network on this bench shares one passphrase, so comparing the
  ; passphrase that came back could not fail whichever slot was read -
  ; every wrong answer is byte-identical to the right one. What is
  ; printed and checked is WHICH SLOT matched and what SSID that slot
  ; holds, which is the only thing that can distinguish them.
  Line("  from stored slot", chosenSlot)
  UartWriteStr("  that slot holds  ")
  PrintSsid(SettingsWifiSlotSsid(chosenSlot), SettingsLength(SettingsWifiSlotSsidKey(chosenSlot)))
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("  under key        ")
  UartWriteStr(SettingsWifiSlotPasswordKey(chosenSlot))
  PrintNl()

  pass = SettingsWifiSlotPassword(chosenSlot)
  passLen = SettingsLength(SettingsWifiSlotPasswordKey(chosenSlot))
  Line("  passphrase bytes", passLen)
  If pass = 0 Or passLen < 8
    UartWriteStr("  THAT SLOT HAS NO USABLE PASSPHRASE. 8 to 63 bytes is the")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  802.11i range and settings.pi4 enforces it on the way in,")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  so a short one here means the key was never set.")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped: no credential ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 8 - THE SECURITY BLOCK, ONE PRINTED VERDICT PER ITEM.
  ;
  ; ITEM 2 IS THE EXPERIMENT. The bare "sup_wpa" existence probe
  ; answered -23 BRCMF_FW_UNSUPPORTED on this board, which by
  ; brcmfmac's rule means no firmware supplicant. But the probe asks
  ; about a DIFFERENT IOVAR from the one a driver writes to turn the
  ; supplicant on, and this line is the direct question: if
  ; bsscfg:sup_wpa = 1 is accepted then the supplicant is there whatever
  ; the probe said, and if it is refused then the host must do PBKDF2
  ; and EAPOL itself and this payload stops here honestly.
  ; ====================================================================
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("-- security --")
  PrintNl()

  verdict = 0
  supGone = 0
  pmkOk = 0
  For i = 0 To #CYW43_SEC_ITEMS - 1
    If i = #CYW43_SEC_PRE
      ; The 100 ms settle and the PMK, between items 4 and 5. Both
      ; reference drivers put a gap here and the longer of the two
      ; margins is the one taken; see Cyw43SecurityWpa2.
      ; ================================================================
      ; THE PMK, AND THE STRUCT SIZE IS THE EXPERIMENT.
      ;
      ; brcmfmac declares 132 bytes and the twin sends 68. On
      ; 2026-08-27 this firmware answered -2 BCME_BADARG to 68 - which
      ; is BADARG and NOT -23 BCME_UNSUPPORTED, so the ioctl EXISTS on
      ; this part and only the argument was wrong. 132 is tried first
      ; because brcmfmac is the driver that works with this image; 68
      ; is tried after, because a second data point costs one ioctl and
      ; "both sizes were refused" is a different finding from "the
      ; other size was right all along".
      ; ================================================================
      cyw43_DelayUs(100000)
      r = Cyw43SetPmk(pass, passLen, #CYW43_PMK_STRUCT_132, 2000)
      UartWriteStr("  PMK 132 bytes (brcmfmac's)         rc ")
      PrintDec(r)
      UartWriteStr("  status ")
      PrintDec(Cyw43IoctlStatus())
      PrintNl()
      If r <> 0
        r = Cyw43SetPmk(pass, passLen, #CYW43_PMK_STRUCT_68, 2000)
        UartWriteStr("  PMK  68 bytes (the twin's)         rc ")
        PrintDec(r)
        UartWriteStr("  status ")
        PrintDec(Cyw43IoctlStatus())
        PrintNl()
      EndIf
      If r = 0
        pmkOk = 1
        UartWriteStr("     ^^ THE FIRMWARE TOOK THE PASSPHRASE. flags = 1 says")
        PrintNl()
        UartWriteStr("     it must run PBKDF2 over these bytes itself, so there")
        PrintNl()
        UartWriteStr("     IS key derivation in here even though bsscfg:sup_wpa")
        PrintNl()
        UartWriteStr("     is not a name it knows.")
        PrintNl()
      EndIf
      If r <> 0
        ; THE PMK IS PART OF THE SUPPLICANT AND FAILS WITH IT. Its
        ; refusal is not a separate fault to stop on when items 2 to 4
        ; have already said there is no supplicant to give a key to -
        ; it is the same fact arriving a fourth time. Counted into
        ; supGone rather than into verdict for exactly that reason.
        supGone = supGone + 1
        UartWriteStr("     ^^ and the PMK with it. -2 is BCME_BADARG, -23 is")
        PrintNl()
        UartWriteStr("     BCME_UNSUPPORTED; either way there is nothing here to")
        PrintNl()
        UartWriteStr("     hand a passphrase to.")
        PrintNl()
      EndIf
      cyw43_DelayUs(3000)
    EndIf

    r = Cyw43SecItem(i, 2000)
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    PrintDec(i)
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(Cyw43SecItemName(i))
    UartWriteStr("   rc ")
    PrintDec(r)
    UartWriteStr("  status ")
    PrintDec(Cyw43IoctlStatus())
    PrintNl()

    If i = #CYW43_SEC_SUPWPA And r <> 0
      UartWriteStr("     ^^ bsscfg:sup_wpa REFUSED. The bare sup_wpa existence")
      PrintNl()
      UartWriteStr("     probe was right: THIS FIRMWARE HAS NO SUPPLICANT.")
      PrintNl()
    EndIf
    If i = #CYW43_SEC_SUPWPA And r = 0
      UartWriteStr("     ^^ ACCEPTED. The firmware CAN run the supplicant,")
      PrintNl()
      UartWriteStr("     whatever the bare sup_wpa probe answered.")
      PrintNl()
    EndIf

    ; ==================================================================
    ; ITEMS 4, 5 AND 6 ARE THE SUPPLICANT AND THEY FAIL TOGETHER OR NOT
    ; AT ALL. They are counted into supGone rather than treated as
    ; gates, and the run CONTINUES.
    ;
    ; WHY CONTINUE, WHEN THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS FILE STOPPED HERE.
    ; Because stopping conflates two things that are not the same:
    ; 802.11 AUTHENTICATION AND ASSOCIATION, which is what the word
    ; "associate" means and which needs no supplicant at all, and the
    ; RSN FOUR-WAY HANDSHAKE, which is key management and does. The
    ; firmware will happily do the first with WSEC and WPA_AUTH set -
    ; those are what put a matching RSN IE in the association request -
    ; and everything after LINK-up is what has nobody to run it.
    ;
    ; So the join goes ahead, and what it measures is exactly how far
    ; association gets on this part. That is a real answer to a real
    ; question. What it must NEVER do is call the result a working
    ; link, which is what Cyw43SetJoinExpectation(NOKEY) is for.
    ; ==================================================================
    If i = #CYW43_SEC_SUPWPA Or i = #CYW43_SEC_EAPVER Or i = #CYW43_SEC_SUPTMO
      If r <> 0
        supGone = supGone + 1
      EndIf
    ElseIf i = #CYW43_SEC_MFP
      ; `mfp` is TOLERATED. A firmware without 802.11w refuses the iovar
      ; and associates perfectly well without it, and stopping a join
      ; over a capability advertisement would turn a missing optional
      ; feature into a milestone failure.
      If r <> 0
        UartWriteStr("     (tolerated - mfp is an advertisement, not a requirement)")
        PrintNl()
      EndIf
    ElseIf r <> 0
      verdict = 1
    EndIf
  Next

  ; The key material is out of both buffers by now - Cyw43SetPmk wipes
  ; cyw43_arg and cyw43_tx unconditionally, on success and failure
  ; alike. Said here because a reader of THIS file should not have to
  ; open the library to find out whether a passphrase is still sitting
  ; in DRAM where `memory 400000 200` would print it.

  If verdict <> 0
    UartWriteStr("  A GATED ITEM WAS REFUSED - one that is NOT part of the")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  supplicant. Not attempting the join; it would fail for a")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  reason already printed above.")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at security ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ; ====================================================================
  ; THE VERDICT ON WHO RUNS THE SUPPLICANT, DECLARED TO THE LIBRARY.
  ;
  ; This is the one place in the run where a fact discovered at run time
  ; changes what the state machine is allowed to conclude, and it is
  ; done explicitly rather than inferred inside cyw43.pi4 - the library
  ; refuses to guess, and this payload is the thing that watched item 2
  ; be refused and therefore knows.
  ; ====================================================================
  ; ====================================================================
  ; TWO FACTS, AND THE PMK IS THE ONE THAT DECIDES.
  ;
  ; The three bsscfg:sup_* iovars are ONE WAY of turning a firmware
  ; supplicant on, and their absence proves only that this firmware does
  ; not know those names. WLC_SET_WSEC_PMK with flags = 1 is a
  ; DIFFERENT and older way, and it is the one that actually hands over
  ; key material: flags = 1 means "these are ASCII, you run PBKDF2".
  ; A firmware that ACCEPTS that has a supplicant whatever it thinks of
  ; the iovar names, so the PMK's verdict outranks theirs.
  ;
  ; The first version of this file got that ordering wrong and declared
  ; NOKEY on the strength of the -23s alone, while the PMK's own answer
  ; was -2 BADARG - a wrong SIZE, not a missing feature.
  ; ====================================================================
  Line("supplicant iovars refused (0 = the firmware knows them)", supGone)
  Line("the firmware took a passphrase (1 = yes)", pmkOk)
  If pmkOk = 1
    Cyw43SetJoinExpectation(#CYW43_JOINEXP_SECURED)
    UartWriteStr("  THE FIRMWARE HAS THE KEY MATERIAL. A full WPA2 join is")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  expected: PSK_SUP status 6 must arrive for this to pass.")
    PrintNl()
  Else
    Cyw43SetJoinExpectation(#CYW43_JOINEXP_NOKEY)
    UartWriteStr("  ================================================")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  NO SUPPLICANT ON EITHER SIDE. WHAT FOLLOWS IS AN")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  802.11 ASSOCIATION PROBE AND NOT A WORKING LINK.")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  ================================================")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  Association and authentication are 802.11; the four-way")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  handshake that follows is RSN key management and there is")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  nothing here to run it. Expect SET_SSID, AUTH and LINK to")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  arrive and NO PSK_SUP, and expect the access point to")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  deauthenticate once its own handshake timer expires.")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  NO DATA FRAME CAN PASS: there is no pairwise key.")
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
  Stamp("security done")

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 9 - THE JOIN
  ; ====================================================================
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("-- join --")
  PrintNl()

  ; ====================================================================
  ; THE LADDER LOOP.
  ;
  ; ONE ITERATION IS EXACTLY THE JOIN THIS FILE ALWAYS DID. Nothing
  ; inside the body was rewritten; it was indented and the two places
  ; that named the single choice now name the rung. A run whose rung 0
  ; succeeds - or whose rung 0 fails in any way probe_FallThrough does
  ; not list - produces the same log it produced before this loop
  ; existed, line for line apart from the two "rung" headers.
  ;
  ; THE PER-RUNG CREDENTIAL IS THE ONE THING A LATER RUNG HAS TO REDO.
  ; The nine security items in section 8 configure the INTERFACE - which
  ; cipher suite and which key-management suite this station offers -
  ; and that is identical for every candidate, so they are sent once and
  ; not re-sent here. The PASSPHRASE is per network, so a rung above 0
  ; looks its slot's credential up and re-sends the PMK before joining.
  ; Section 8 already did that for rung 0, including its two-struct-size
  ; experiment and all of its narration, and repeating that experiment
  ; three more times would bury the finding it exists to report.
  ;
  ; chosen AND chosenSlot ARE UPDATED AS THE LADDER MOVES, so section 10
  ; below - the read-back that proves which access point actually
  ; answered - checks the rung that was last attempted, without knowing
  ; the ladder exists.
  ; ====================================================================
  rung = 0
  attempts = 0
  fellThrough = 0
  verdict = #CYW43_JOIN_FAILED

  While rung < rungs
    chosen = ladderRow[rung]
    chosenSlot = ladderSlot[rung]

    UartWriteStr("  --- rung ")
    PrintDec(rung)
    UartWriteStr(" of ")
    PrintDec(rungs)
    UartWriteStr("  slot ")
    PrintDec(chosenSlot)
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    PrintSsid(Cyw43ScanSsid(chosen), Cyw43ScanSsidLen(chosen))
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    PrintBssid(@cyw43_bssBssid[0] + chosen * 6)
    PrintNl()
    UartDrain()

    If rung > 0
      ; A LATER RUNG'S CREDENTIAL. Looked up the same way section 8
      ; looked up rung 0's, and refused on the same terms: 8 to 63
      ; bytes is the 802.11i range and settings.pi4 enforces it on the
      ; way in, so a short one here means the key was never set.
      pass = SettingsWifiSlotPassword(chosenSlot)
      passLen = SettingsLength(SettingsWifiSlotPasswordKey(chosenSlot))
      If pass = 0 Or passLen < 8
        UartWriteStr("      that slot has no usable passphrase - skipping this rung")
        PrintNl()
        rung = rung + 1
        Continue
      EndIf

      ; The same 100 ms settle and the same struct-size order as
      ; section 8. Quietly this time: the experiment was reported once
      ; and the answer will not have changed between two rungs.
      cyw43_DelayUs(100000)
      r = Cyw43SetPmk(pass, passLen, #CYW43_PMK_STRUCT_132, 2000)
      If r <> 0
        r = Cyw43SetPmk(pass, passLen, #CYW43_PMK_STRUCT_68, 2000)
      EndIf
      Line("      PMK for this rung (0 = taken)", r)
      cyw43_DelayUs(3000)
    EndIf

    attempts = attempts + 1
    r = Cyw43JoinStart(Cyw43ScanSsid(chosen), Cyw43ScanSsidLen(chosen), 2000)
    Line("Cyw43JoinStart (want 0)", r)
    Line("  ioctl status   ", Cyw43IoctlStatus())
    If r <> 0
      UartWriteStr("  THE REQUEST ITSELF WAS REFUSED, so no events will follow.")
      PrintNl()
      UartWriteStr("=== stopped starting the join ===")
      PrintNl()
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf

  ; EVERY EVENT IS PRINTED AS IT ARRIVES, with its raw numbers. Nothing
  ; on this part has ever been measured, so the ORDER of these lines is
  ; itself the finding - which is why this loop prints before it acts on
  ; the verdict rather than summarising afterwards.
    slices = 0
    verdict = #CYW43_JOIN_RUNNING
    While slices < #JOIN_MAX_SLICES
      verdict = Cyw43JoinPoll(#JOIN_SLICE_MS)
      slices = slices + 1
      If Cyw43EventNew() <> 0
        UartWriteStr("  [t+")
        PrintDec(NowMs() - startMs)
        UartWriteStr(" ms] event ")
        PrintDec(Cyw43JoinEventType())
        UartWriteStr(" ")
        UartWriteStr(Cyw43EventName(Cyw43JoinEventType()))
        UartWriteStr("  status ")
        PrintDec(Cyw43JoinEventStatus())
        UartWriteStr("  reason ")
        PrintDec(Cyw43JoinEventReason())
        UartWriteStr("  flags $")
        PutHexN(Cyw43JoinEventFlags(), 4)
        UartWriteStr("  state $")
        PutHexN(Cyw43JoinState(), 4)
        PrintNl()
        ; AN ASSOC EVENT THAT FAILED CARRIES THE ACCESS POINT'S OWN 802.11
        ; STATUS CODE IN ITS REASON FIELD, and it is the only place in the
        ; whole run where the other end explains itself. Printed here, in
        ; words, immediately under the raw numbers.
        If Cyw43JoinEventType() = #CYW43_EV_ASSOC
          If Cyw43JoinEventStatus() <> 0
            UartWriteStr("        the AP said: ")
            UartWriteStr(Cyw43AssocStatusText(Cyw43JoinEventReason()))
            PrintNl()
          EndIf
        EndIf
        UartDrain()
      EndIf
      If verdict <> #CYW43_JOIN_RUNNING
        Break
      EndIf
    Wend

    Line("poll slices used ", slices)
    Line("join verdict (1 = JOINED)", verdict)
    HexLine("accumulator      ", Cyw43JoinState(), 4)
    Line("SET_SSID status 0 seen", Cyw43JoinSsidOk())
    Line("events seen      ", Cyw43EventsSeen())
    Line("events DROPPED   ", Cyw43EventsDropped())
    Line("ICV errors       ", Cyw43IcvErrors())
    If slices >= #JOIN_MAX_SLICES
      UartWriteStr("  THE CALLER'S OWN CEILING FIRED, which means the library's")
      PrintNl()
      UartWriteStr("  guards did not. That is a bug in Cyw43JoinPoll, not in the")
      PrintNl()
      UartWriteStr("  radio.")
      PrintNl()
    EndIf
    ; WHAT "JOINED" MEANS HERE, IN WORDS, EVERY TIME. The library owns
    ; the sentence (Cyw43JoinedMeansText) so that this log and the state
    ; machine cannot disagree about what was proved.
    UartWriteStr("  JOINED would mean: ")
    UartWriteStr(Cyw43JoinedMeansText())
    PrintNl()

    If verdict <> #CYW43_JOIN_JOINED
      Line("join failure class", Cyw43JoinFail())
      UartWriteStr("  ")
      UartWriteStr(Cyw43JoinFailText())
      PrintNl()
      Line("  phase reached  ", Cyw43JoinPhase())
    Else
      Stamp("ASSOCIATED")
    EndIf

    ; ==================================================================
    ; THE FALL-THROUGH DECISION, AND THE FOUR REASONS IT CAN SAY NO.
    ;
    ; Printed in full every time, because a ladder that stops without
    ; saying why is worse than no ladder: the log would look identical
    ; to the old single-choice behaviour and nobody would know which
    ; they were reading.
    ; ==================================================================
    If verdict = #CYW43_JOIN_JOINED
      Break
    EndIf

    If probe_FallThrough(Cyw43JoinFail()) = 0
      UartWriteStr("  LADDER STOPS: this failure is not one another network")
      PrintNl()
      UartWriteStr("  would fix. See probe_FallThrough for the three that are.")
      PrintNl()
      Break
    EndIf

    If rung + 1 >= rungs
      UartWriteStr("  LADDER STOPS: that was the last rung.")
      PrintNl()
      Break
    EndIf

    If (NowMs() - startMs) > #JOIN_LADDER_BUDGET_MS
      Line("  LADDER STOPS on the clock, elapsed ms", NowMs() - startMs)
      UartWriteStr("  The deadman gets the rest of the budget.")
      PrintNl()
      Break
    EndIf

    ; DISASSOCIATE BEFORE THE NEXT RUNG. Not tidiness: the twin's bench
    ; measured attempt-1 success going from 4.3% to 75.3% across 144
    ; reset-to-reset cycles when a clean teardown preceded the retry,
    ; and the keying-stall failure MODE disappeared rather than becoming
    ; rarer. cyw43.pi4's Cyw43Disassoc carries the citation. Here it
    ; also clears whatever half-state the refusing access point left.
    r = Cyw43Disassoc(2000)
    Line("  disassociate before the next rung (0 = ok)", r)
    r = Cyw43DrainEvents(200)
    Line("  events drained ", r)
    fellThrough = fellThrough + 1
    rung = rung + 1
  Wend

  ; attempts, NOT rung + 1: a rung whose slot turned out to have no
  ; usable passphrase is skipped without an association attempt, and
  ; counting it would overstate what the radio was asked to do.
  Line("rungs attempted  ", attempts)
  Line("fall-throughs    ", fellThrough)

  If verdict <> #CYW43_JOIN_JOINED
    ; THE READ-BACKS RUN ANYWAY. A join that failed may still have been
    ; associated for part of a second - AUTH and LINK arriving and then
    ; a DEAUTH is exactly the supplicant-less signature - and
    ; WLC_GET_BSSID answering with an address at any point is the
    ; evidence that association itself worked. Refusing to look because
    ; the verdict was FAILED would throw away the finding.
    UartWriteStr("  === NOT A COMPLETED JOIN - reading the link back anyway ===")
    PrintNl()
  EndIf

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 10 - THE PROOF. Not what the state machine thinks - what the
  ; firmware says when asked.
  ; ====================================================================
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("-- proof --")
  PrintNl()

  r = Cyw43ReadBssid(2000)
  Line("WLC_GET_BSSID says associated (want 1)", r)
  If r = 1
    UartWriteStr("  negotiated BSSID  ")
    PrintBssid(@cyw43_linkBssid[0])
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  we chose          ")
    PrintBssid(@cyw43_bssBssid[0] + chosen * 6)
    PrintNl()

    ; THE COMPARISON IS THE POINT OF THE WHOLE SELECTION. Joining
    ; something called by the right name is not the same as joining the
    ; access point that was chosen, and on a network with more than one
    ; AP broadcasting one SSID the two routinely differ.
    bssidOk = 1
    For j = 0 To 5
      If Cyw43LinkBssidByte(j) <> Cyw43ScanBssidByte(chosen, j)
        bssidOk = 0
      EndIf
    Next
    Line("  BSSID MATCHES THE ONE WE CHOSE (want 1)", bssidOk)
    If bssidOk = 0
      UartWriteStr("  A DIFFERENT ACCESS POINT ANSWERED. That is not a failure")
      PrintNl()
      UartWriteStr("  of the link - it is a failure of the selection to bind.")
      PrintNl()
      UartWriteStr("  WLC_SET_SSID joins by NAME; pinning a BSSID needs the")
      PrintNl()
      UartWriteStr("  `join` iovar, which cyw43.pi4 does not build. See the")
      PrintNl()
      UartWriteStr("  head of section 8 there.")
      PrintNl()
    EndIf
  EndIf

  r = Cyw43ReadSsid(2000)
  Line("WLC_GET_SSID rc  ", r)
  If r = 0
    UartWriteStr("  negotiated SSID   ")
    PrintSsid(Cyw43LinkSsid(), Cyw43LinkSsidLen())
    PrintNl()
    matched = 1
    If Cyw43LinkSsidLen() <> Cyw43ScanSsidLen(chosen)
      matched = 0
    Else
      For j = 0 To Cyw43LinkSsidLen() - 1
        If PeekA(Cyw43LinkSsid() + j) <> PeekA(Cyw43ScanSsid(chosen) + j)
          matched = 0
        EndIf
      Next
    EndIf
    Line("  SSID matches (want 1)", matched)
  EndIf

  r = Cyw43ReadChannel(2000)
  Line("WLC_GET_CHANNEL rc", r)
  If r = 0
    Line("  negotiated channel", Cyw43LinkChannel())
    Line("  the scan said     ", Cyw43ScanChannel(chosen))
  EndIf

  r = Cyw43ReadRssi(2000)
  Line("WLC_GET_RSSI rc  ", r)
  If r = 0
    Line("  link RSSI dBm   ", Cyw43LinkRssi())
    If probe_RssiSane(Cyw43LinkRssi()) = 0
      UartWriteStr("  THAT IS NOT A PLAUSIBLE dBm READING - see probe_RssiSane.")
      PrintNl()
    EndIf
  EndIf

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 10b - THE THREE QUESTIONS A HOST SUPPLICANT NEEDS ANSWERED, AND THEY
  ;       ALL COST LESS THAN A SECOND EACH.
  ;
  ; The firmware build string already says there is no in-dongle
  ; supplicant - see the head of section 10 in cyw43.pi4 for the two
  ; blobs side by side. What it does NOT say is whether the three
  ; things a HOST supplicant depends on are there:
  ;
  ;   1. does the AP actually start a four-way handshake with us, and
  ;      does the firmware pass EAPOL up to the host as a data frame?
  ;      THIS IS THE ONE THAT DECIDES EVERYTHING. If message 1 never
  ;      arrives there is nothing to answer and no amount of correct
  ;      arithmetic helps.
  ;   2. what RSN information element did the association request
  ;      actually carry? Message 2 has to repeat it byte for byte.
  ;   3. does the BARE `sup_wpa` spelling - brcmfmac's, cfg80211.c:2477
  ;      - answer differently from the `bsscfg:` one the last bench
  ;      sent? Expected no. Asked anyway, because it is one ioctl and
  ;      it closes the question.
  ; ====================================================================
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("-- what a host supplicant would need --")
  PrintNl()

  r = Cyw43MacAddress(@ourMac[0], 2000)
  Line("cur_etheraddr rc ", r)
  If r = 0
    UartWriteStr("  our MAC          ")
    PrintBssid(@ourMac[0])
    PrintNl()
  EndIf

  ; --- 3, first, because it is the cheapest -------------------------
  r = Cyw43SupWpaEnable(2000)
  UartWriteStr("  sup_wpa = 1 (BARE name, brcmfmac's)   rc ")
  PrintDec(r)
  UartWriteStr("  status ")
  PrintDec(Cyw43IoctlStatus())
  PrintNl()
  If r = 0
    UartWriteStr("     ^^ ACCEPTED. Everything below is unnecessary - the")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("     firmware will run the handshake. Give it the PMK.")
    PrintNl()
  Else
    UartWriteStr("     ^^ refused, as the build string predicted. -23 is")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("     BCME_UNSUPPORTED: the name is not in this image.")
    PrintNl()
  EndIf

  ; --- 2 - what went out in the association request -----------------
  r = Cyw43GetAssocReqIes(160, 2000)
  Line("assoc_req_ies rc ", r)
  If r = 0
    n = Cyw43ReplyLen()
    Line("  bytes of IEs    ", n)
    p = Cyw43ReplyPtr()
    ; Walk the elements. id, len, body - and print the RSN one ($30)
    ; in full, because those exact bytes are what message 2 must carry.
    i = 0
    While i + 2 <= n
      k = PeekA(p + i + 1)
      If i + 2 + k > n
        UartWriteStr("  IE RUNS PAST THE REPLY at offset ")
        PrintDec(i)
        PrintNl()
        Break
      EndIf
      UartWriteStr("  IE id ")
      PrintDec(PeekA(p + i))
      UartWriteStr(" len ")
      PrintDec(k)
      If PeekA(p + i) = 48
        UartWriteStr("   <- RSN:  ")
        For j = 0 To k + 1
          PutHexN(PeekA(p + i + j), 2)
          UartWrite(32)
        Next
      EndIf
      PrintNl()
      i = i + 2 + k
    Wend
    UartDrain()
  EndIf

  ; --- 1 - THE DECIDING QUESTION ------------------------------------
  ; EtherType $888E is 802.1X. The firmware passes it up in the clear
  ; before any key is installed - that is the whole point of the
  ; controlled/uncontrolled port split - so if the AP is talking to us
  ; at all, message 1 lands here as an ordinary 802.3 frame on SDPCM
  ; channel 2.
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("  listening for EAPOL, ")
  PrintDec(#EAPOL_LISTEN_MS)
  UartWriteStr(" ms ...")
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()

  eapolLen = 0
  eapolMs = NowMs()
  While (NowMs() - eapolMs) < #EAPOL_LISTEN_MS
    n = Cyw43Receive(100)
    If n >= 14
      p = Cyw43ReceivePtr()
      If PeekA(p + 12) = $88 And PeekA(p + 13) = $8E
        eapolLen = n
        Break
      EndIf
      UartWriteStr("  a data frame, ")
      PrintDec(n)
      UartWriteStr(" bytes, ethertype $")
      PutHexN(PeekA(p + 12), 2)
      PutHexN(PeekA(p + 13), 2)
      UartWriteStr(" - not 802.1X")
      PrintNl()
      UartDrain()
    EndIf
  Wend

  If eapolLen = 0
    UartWriteStr("  NO EAPOL IN ")
    PrintDec(#EAPOL_LISTEN_MS)
    UartWriteStr(" ms. Either the association never completed, or")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  the AP gave up before we listened, or the firmware is not")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  passing 802.1X frames to the host. The rx data frame")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  counter below separates the last of those from the others.")
    PrintNl()
  Else
    p = Cyw43ReceivePtr()
    UartWriteStr("  *** EAPOL MESSAGE, ")
    PrintDec(eapolLen)
    UartWriteStr(" bytes ***")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("    from        ")
    PrintBssid(p + 6)
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("    to          ")
    PrintBssid(p)
    PrintNl()
    Line("    1X version ", PeekA(p + 14))
    Line("    1X type    ", PeekA(p + 15))
    Line("    1X body len", (PeekA(p + 16) << 8) | PeekA(p + 17))
    If PeekA(p + 15) = 3 And eapolLen >= 113
      Line("    descriptor ", PeekA(p + 18))
      UartWriteStr("    key info   $")
      PutHexN((PeekA(p + 19) << 8) | PeekA(p + 20), 4)
      PrintNl()
      Line("    key length ", (PeekA(p + 21) << 8) | PeekA(p + 22))
      Line("    key data ln", (PeekA(p + 111) << 8) | PeekA(p + 112))
      UartWriteStr("    THAT IS MESSAGE 1 OF THE FOUR-WAY HANDSHAKE. Key")
      PrintNl()
      UartWriteStr("    descriptor version is the low three bits of key info;")
      PrintNl()
      UartWriteStr("    2 means HMAC-SHA1-128 MIC and AES key wrap.")
      PrintNl()
    EndIf
  EndIf
  UartDrain()

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 11 - the counters, whether or not any of it worked
  ; ====================================================================
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("-- transport counters --")
  PrintNl()
  Line("rx frames        ", Cyw43RxFrames())
  Line("rx data frames   ", Cyw43RxDataFrames())
  Line("tx data frames   ", Cyw43TxFrames())
  Line("bad headers      ", Cyw43RxBadHdr())
  Line("too short        ", Cyw43RxShort())
  Line("other channels   ", Cyw43RxOther())
  Line("other events     ", Cyw43EvOther())
  Line("bad event prefix ", Cyw43EvBadIsland())
  Line("empty events     ", Cyw43EvEmpty())
  Line("credit stalls    ", Cyw43Stalls())
  Line("duplicates folded", Cyw43ScanDuplicates())
  PrintNl()

  Stamp("done")
  UartWriteStr("=== done ===")
  PrintNl()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Main()
